#{[17592186124874 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}(9)/((2 - x)^{5})$."] [17592186124902 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}\\frac{3x^{4} + 1}{-3x^{4} - 2x^{3}}$."] [17592186124911 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2x+5}{2{x}^{3}+2x+6} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(2+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{2+0+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$"] [17592186124917 :question/problem "![](images/7c/7c9f40ccd9232d33005e81a40273e993458d248366ca90e41c9b7e403afb5a46.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x)$. Find $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}f(x)$."] [17592186124918 :question/problem "![](images/c8/c818664c51129132433ada3ca470c6a1a99753ed59a3fd9e4429898200f4fc3a.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n1. $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}f(x) =-3$\n2. $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}f(x) = 0$\n3. $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}f(x) =-3$"] [17592186124919 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x+4}{6-15x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{4}{x})}{(\\frac{6}{x}-\\frac{15x}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3+\\frac{4}{x})}{(\\frac{6}{x}-15)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+0}{0-15} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-\\frac{1}{5}.$"] [17592186124921 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{12x-3{x}^{4}}{3{x}^{2}+7} \\\\  & \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{12x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{12}{x}-3{x}^{2})}{(3+\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{0-3{x}^{2}}{3+0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [17592186124937 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(1,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}}{{1}^{3}+{1}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124938 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}=\\frac{0\\cdot 0-0\\cdot {0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x)-x(x)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2x(x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-x}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-0}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y=-x$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(-x)-x(-x)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2x(-x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(1+x) \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $1$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186124952 :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_{x \\to  2}(5)/(2(x - 2)^{8})$."] [17592186124953 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-2,3)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-2,3)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2,3)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy} & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-2)^{2}+(-2)\\cdot {3}^{2}}{(-2)^{2}+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-18}{4-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10}{-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124986 :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_{x \\to  2}\\frac{1}{x - 2}$."] [17592186125082 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}\\frac{-15}{x^{3} - 2}$."] [17592186125089 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-6}{{x}^{2}-x+2} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{({x}^{2}-0)}{(1-0+0)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186125145 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  0^{-}}\\frac{2}{3x}$?"] [17592186125146 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}\\frac{2x^{3} - 13}{x + 11}$."] [17592186125159 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  10}(\\frac{x}{2} + 1)$."] [17592186125161 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  0^{+}}2\\csc x$."] [17592186125162 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  (π/2)^{-}}\\sec x$."] [17592186125163 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  0^{-}}\\cot (4x)$."] [17592186125166 :question/problem "![](images/6c/6cbafdc56d756e9806f36c71ae3b479af2001ebb3632b54a1ef2d7bcccfdcb0d.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n1. $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}f(x) = - ∞$\n2. $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}f(x) = ∞$\n3. $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}f(x) = - ∞$"] [17592186125173 :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f8af69e677998b30e52d714b9c5b43e1455e806f3ee525e89dc600c0a98f701.png)\n\nThe figure below shows the graphs of $y = f(x)$ and $y = g(x)$. Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  π}(3f(x) - g(x))$."] [17592186125174 :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}\\frac{24x^{5}}{3x^{9}}$."] [17592186125199 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{2}{x^{3}}\\right)$?"] [17592186125200 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}\\frac{1}{x^{2} + 1}$."] [17592186125201 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186125202 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  π}\\sec \\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)$."] [17592186125203 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  π}5\\cot (x - \\frac{π}{2})$."] [17592186125220 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-{y}^{2}z}=\\frac{{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{{2}^{3}-{2}^{2}\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-{y}^{2}z} & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{z({z}^{2}-{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{1}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{1}{z} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [17592186125221 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{1+xy}-1}=\\frac{{0}^{2}-0}{\\sqrt{1+0\\cdot 1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{1+xy}-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{(\\sqrt{1+xy}-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{x(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{(\\sqrt{1+xy})^{2}-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{x(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{xy} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{y} & =\\frac{(0-1)(\\sqrt{1+0\\cdot 1}+1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{1} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"] [17592186125222 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot 0+0\\cdot {0}^{2}}{{0}^{3}+0\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{3}-{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186125223 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{xyz-z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot {2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{xyz-z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{yz(x-y)}{-(x-y)(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{yz}{-x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{yz}{-x-y} & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)}{-2-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [17592186125224 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (4,4)}\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}}=\\frac{4-4}{\\sqrt{4}-\\sqrt{4}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (4,4)}\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (4,4)}\\frac{(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (4,4)}\\frac{(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-(\\sqrt{y})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (4,4)}\\frac{(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (4,4)}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (4,4)}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}) & =\\sqrt{4}+\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $4.$"] [17592186125714 :tutorial/content "Until now, we have considered limits at individual points: \n\n$\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} f(x)$ is the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ approaches the point $a.$ \n\nHowever, the idea of a limit extends beyond individual points. For instance, it might also be the case that $f(x)$ approaches some value as $x$ gets bigger and bigger, growing without bound.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x} + 1$ whose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/71/71fdfd68ce32097cac17da935f0422b471b28d804f4108c62783d0ffc1e96f24.png)\n\nOn the right side of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets bigger and bigger, $f(x)$ approaches the horizontal asymptote $y=1.$ In other words, as $x$ increases to infinity ($\\infty$), the value of $f(x)$ approaches a limit of $1.$ We can write this symbolically as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty } f(x) = 1.\n$$\n\nLikewise, on the left side of the graph, as $x$ decreases to negative infinity ($-\\infty$), the value of $f(x)$ approaches the same horizontal asymptote $y=1.$ Consequently, we also have that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty } f(x) = 1.\n$$"] [17592186125718 :tutorial/content "The **sum rule** states that the limit of the sum of two functions equals the sum of the limits (provided that those limits exist).\n\nMore precisely, if\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}g(x)=K\n$$\n\n, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  a}(f(x) + g(x)) &= \\lim_{x \\to  a}f(x) + \\lim_{x \\to  a}g(x) \\\\\n&= L + K\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\,-3} \\left(5x^2+2x\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can first apply the sum rule, followed by the constant rule, and compute the limits of the power functions.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  - 3}(5x^{2} + 2x) &= \\lim_{x \\to  - 3}5x^{2} + \\lim_{x \\to  - 3}2x \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot lim_(x → - 3)x^{2} + 2 \\cdot lim_(x → - 3)x \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot (-3)^{2} + 2 \\cdot (-3) \\\\\n&= 45 - 6 \\\\\n&= 39\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere is a shortcut: in general, to find the limit of a polynomial $f(x)$ at some point $x=c$, all we need to do is evaluate the polynomial at $c$.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c} f(x) = f(c)\n$$\n\n**Warning!** If one of $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)$ or $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}g(x)$ exists while the other does not exist, then $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}\\Bigl(f(x)+g(x) \\Bigr)$ does not exist either."] [17592186125719 :tutorial/content "How do we calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to-\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)?\n$$ To find out, let's look at the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea736c37f855415e54d8b3a3775fab07159b5de4a368bee45bba13ae261372da.png)\n\nWe see that the curve approaches zero as $x$ approaches positive infinity. The same is true as $x$ approaches negative infinity. So, we write \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)=0 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\lim_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe same is true for $\\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ where $n$ is *any* positive integer. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)=0\\, & \\text{and}\\,\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)=0 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)=0\\, & \\text{and}\\,\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so on. This is because as the size of $x$ increases, the size of $\\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ decreases."] [17592186125720 :tutorial/content "Let's again look at the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/e8/e8b24529b70e32a32eb45fc9ce5d907ca74020a940b46fbf63b5386bf2d8cd9d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0^-} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = -\\infty \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0^+} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = \\infty.\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not the same, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nAny function of the form $y = \\dfrac{1}{x^n},$ where $n$ is an *odd* natural number, has the same shape as $y = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ and consequently the limit behavior is the same.  For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)=-\\infty , & \\,\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)=\\infty , \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\right)=-\\infty , & \\,\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\right)=\\infty , \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so on. For all of the above, the limit of the function does not exist at $x=0$ because the left and right limits are not the same."] [17592186125721 :tutorial/content "Now let's look at the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}.$\n\n![](images/b7/b75253dcc54e0c833995611513b17cb8563249cb5aeb32078508d2c43556313b.png)\n\nWe see that as we approach $0$ from both sides, the function grows rapidly without bound. So \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0^-}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right) = \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0^+} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)= \\infty,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)= \\infty.\n$$\n\nAny function of the form $y = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ (where $n$ is an *even* natural number) has the same shape as $y = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^n}\\right)= \\infty, \\qquad n=2,4,6,\\ldots\n$$"] [17592186125731 :tutorial/content "We can extend our knowledge of limits to multivariable functions. We'll first consider the two-variable case. \n\nWe denote $\\mathbf{x}=(x,y)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_0 = (a,b)$ as a particular, fixed vector. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined in some neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ \n\nThen, the notation\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{x}_0} f (x,y) = L\n$$\n\nmeans that the value of $f$ can be made as close as we want to $L,$ provided that $\\mathbf x$ is sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{x}_0.$\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- This definition of a limit also extends to functions of more than two variables in the natural way.\n\n- All of the rules set out by the algebra of limits are valid for functions of several variables.\n\nWe say that $f(x,y)$ is **continuous** at $(a,b)$ if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (a,b)} f (x,y) = f(a,b).\n$$\n\nSo, if a function is continuous at $(a,b),$ then the limit can be evaluated by simply plugging $(x,y) = (a,b)$ into the function.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{xy}{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nThis function is continuous for all $(x,y)$ with the exception of $(0,0)$ where the denominator vanishes. Since the point $(2,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(x,y) = (2,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186125742 :tutorial/content "From single-variable calculus, if a function $y=f(x)$ is differentiable at a point, then it is continuous at that point.\n\nBut for a multivariable function, even if all partial derivatives are defined at a certain point, it **does not mean** that the function is continuous at that point!\n\nThe reason why partial differentiability does not imply continuity in the case of a multivariable function is that\n\n- the existence of the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y$ depend only on the behavior of $f$ parallel to the $x$- and $y$-axes, whereas\n\n- the continuity of $f$ depends on its behavior as we approach $(x_0,y_0)$ from any direction.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following multivariable function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{x y}{ {x}^{2} + {y}^{2}}} , \\hspace{1em} & ( x , y ) \\ne  ( 0 , 0 ) \\\\ 0 , & ( x , y ) = ( 0 , 0 )\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe function $f$ is defined at the point $(0,0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf(0,0)=0\n$$\n\nLikewise, the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y$ are both defined at the point $(0,0).$ We can compute them using the limit definition of the partial derivative, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,0) & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[f(0+h,0)-f(0,0)] \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[\\frac{(h)(0)}{{h}^{2}+{0}^{2}}-0] \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[0-0] \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,0) & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[f(0,0+h)-f(0,0)] \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[\\frac{(0)(h)}{{0}^{2}+{h}^{2}}-0] \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[0-0] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the partial derivatives both exist at $(0,0).$ We will now show that the function is not continuous at $(0,0).$\n\nFor the function $f$ to be continuous at $(0,0),$ the limit of the function must be equal to the function value, regardless of the path taken. So, we must have\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{(x,y) \\to (0,0)}  f(x,y) = f(0,0) =0.\n$$\n\nHowever, this is not the case here. When we evaluate the limit along the path $y=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (0,0)\\,\\,y=x}f(x,y) & =\\lim_{y\\to 0}\\frac{x\\cdot x}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{y\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{y\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function $f(x,y)$ is *not* continuous at the point $(0,0),$ even though both of its partial derivatives exist there."] [17592186125743 :tutorial/content "To calculate the limit at infinity of a rational function, such as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{x^4}{x^5-x^2}\\right),\n$$\n\n we apply the following procedure:\n\n**Step 1:** Divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the *denominator*.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}-{x}^{2}}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}÷{x}^{5}}{({x}^{5}-{x}^{2})÷{x}^{5}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}}}{\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{\\frac{1}{x}}{1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Apply the rule that if $n$ is any positive integer then $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^n}\\right)=0.\n$$ Evaluate the remaining expression to obtain the answer.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}-{x}^{2}}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{\\frac{1}{x}}{1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186125744 :tutorial/content "Not every function $f(x)$ levels off to approach an asymptote as $x$ approaches infinity.\n\nInstead, a function $f(x)$ might get bigger and bigger, increasing without bound, as shown in the graph below.\n\n![](images/99/99d75e971e3ac33723bd6a012806af08ae69a5677a7a44553569bdf6706a81ac.png)\n\nIn this case, we say that the limit of the function is infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x) = \\infty\n$$"] [17592186125745 :tutorial/content "Consider the graph of $y=\\sec(x)$ as shown below. We will use this graph to work out limits involving $\\sec(x).$\n\n![](images/4d/4d17509953509a496f1f44aeab8e449a2491ee8853f69a5eaf461aca2d77cf88.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that for any point that is not an asymptote, the limit of $\\sec(x)$ is equal to the value of $\\sec(x)$ at that point.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x) &=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(a),\\,a\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt each asymptote, the one-sided infinite limits exist. For example:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi/2)^{-}}\\sec(x)=\\infty,\\qquad \\lim_{x\\to (\\pi/2)^{+}}\\sec(x)=-\\infty.\n$$\n\nHowever, because the left and right-sided limits are not equal, the overall limit at each asymptote does not exist.\n\n$$\n\\lim_ {x \\to a} \\sec \\left(x\\right)=\\text{DNE}, \\qquad a= \\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pm \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\ldots\n$$"] [17592186125746 :tutorial/content "Finally, let's look at the graph $y= \\cot (x).$ We will use this graph to work out limits involving $\\cot(x).$\n\n![](images/c4/c4812e12724eee3c90d7d040356f4b3c37aefe0816877d37a83abd8d8635fd58.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that for any point that is not an asymptote, the limit of $\\cot(x)$ is equal to the value of $\\cot(x)$ at that point.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to a} \\cot (x)=\\cot\\left(a\\right), \\qquad a\\neq 0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm 2\\pi,\\ldots\n$$\n\nAt each asymptote, the one-sided limits exist. For example:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 0^{+}} \\cot (x) = \\infty, \\qquad \\lim_{x \\to 0^{-}} \\cot (x) = -\\infty.\n$$\n\nHowever, because the left and right-sided limits are not equal, the overall limit at each asymptote does not exist.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to a} \\cot (x)=\\text{DNE}, \\qquad a= 0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm 2\\pi,\\ldots\n$$"] [17592186125747 :tutorial/content "Now, consider the graph of $y=\\csc{x}$ as shown below. We will use this graph to work out limits involving $\\csc x.$\n\n![](images/c0/c028d27e8cc4154587fde1455a25690b64b9642b36b467afe6c4e9c3e1e2f0a7.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that for any point that is not an asymptote, the limit of $\\csc x$ is equal to the value of $\\csc x$ at that point.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x &=\\operatorname{csc}⁡a,\\,a\\ne 0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt each asymptote, the one-sided limits exist. For example:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-}\\csc(x)=-\\infty, \\quad \\lim_{x\\to 0^+}\\csc(x)=\\infty.\n$$\n\nHowever, because the left and right-sided limits are not equal, the overall limit at each asymptote does not exist.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\csc(x)=\\text{DNE}, \\qquad a = 0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm 2\\pi\\ldots\n$$"] [17592186125748 :tutorial/content "The horizontal asymptotes of a rational function $y=f(x)$ coincide with the infinite limits of $f(x).$\n\nFor example, we can use the same procedure as before to find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x) = \\dfrac{x+1}{x^2+2}.$ As a result, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(1+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the horizontal asymptote of the graph $y=f(x)$ is $y=0$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27c77f2d6a06bad338982d000f916811201c45e9b16713caf40cc4d5bcf911bd.png)"] [17592186125754 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we may need to simplify a limit before we can evaluate it. \n\nFor example, let's consider the limit\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2}.\n$$\n\nDirect substitution causes this limit to take the indeterminate form $\\dfrac 00$ because the numerator and denominator both evaluate to zero at the given point. \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2} = \\dfrac{(2)^2 + 2(2)(-1)}{(2)(-1) + 2(-1)^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by factoring the numerator and denominator and then canceling a common factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy}{xy+2{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{y(x+2y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2} = -2.\n$$"] [17592186125809 :tutorial/content "However, it is often easy to show that a limit does not exist for a multivariable function. All we have to do is find two paths on which the limit evaluates to different results. \n\nAs an example, let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)} \\dfrac{y}{x+y}\n$$\n\nClearly, direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00.$ Let's compute the limit along the paths $y=x$ and $y=x^2.$ We will see that the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{y}{x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{x+x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{1+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{y}{x +y} = \\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186125859 :tutorial/content "Deducing the range of an absolute value function using graph transformations will get us the correct answer. However, there is a faster way.\n\nLet's once again calculate the range of the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mid x+1 \\mid + 2\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that the range of $\\mid x \\mid$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mid x \\mid \\ge 0\n$$\n\nWe know that horizontal shifts do not affect the range. So,\n\n$$\n\\mid x+1 \\mid \\ge 0\n$$\n\nFinally, adding $2$ to the above inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+1| & \\ge 0 \\\\ |x+1|+2 & \\ge 2 \\\\ f(x) & \\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $f(x) \\in [2,\\infty)$, as before."] [17592186125866 :tutorial/content "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function of two variables. The **partial derivative of** $\\boldsymbol{f}$ **with respect to** $\\boldsymbol x$ is computed by taking the derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ using the usual methods, *while treating $y$ as a constant.*\n\nThe partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ has the following notation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f} {\\partial x}\n$$\n\nThis partial derivative is defined as the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{f(x+h,y)-f(x,y)}{h}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's worth taking a moment to compare this to the derivative of a single-variable function $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also define the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y,$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f} {\\partial y}\t\t = \\lim_{h \\to 0}\\left(\\dfrac {f (x ,y + h) - f(x, y)} {h}\\right)\n$$\n\nIn this case, we'd differentiate $f$ with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ as a constant.\n\nWe also have the following notations for partial derivatives:\n\n$$\nf_x =  \\dfrac {\\partial f} {\\partial x}, \\qquad  f_y = \\dfrac {\\partial f} {\\partial y}\n$$"] [17592186128900 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x),$ defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}x+3, & x<0 \\\\ 0, & x=0 \\\\ 3-x, & x>0,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^+} f(x)=\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^-} f(x)\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)=f(0)\n$$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0$"] [17592186128900 :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(0)=0$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(x+3)=3, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(3-x)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3. \\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is $$\n\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3,\n$$ while the value of the function is $f(0)=0.$ So, statement II is false and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)\\neq f(0)\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=0,$ and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."] [17592186128928 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 2} \\sqrt{x-2}.\n$$"] [17592186128928 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\sqrt{x-2}\n$$\n\n. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(2) &= \\sqrt{2 - 2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{0} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the graph of $y=f(x)$ \"starts\" at $x=2$. Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$ as follows:\n\n![](images/a4/a4a64caff7a8b69454ffc8b93a1baad18bf3d93093b3c85a7c4fc3cb9762a2b4.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we notice that the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = \\textrm{DNE}\n$$\n\nwhile the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = 0\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are not equal, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 2} f(x)= \\textrm{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186128929 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} \\sqrt{3-x}.\n$$"] [17592186128929 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\sqrt{3-x}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n![](images/d3/d3960d69c67ce1dbf731975ec1058f590ae6cfd61a3c3b04cb5632ce2eaf68aa.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to -\\infty} f(x)= \\infty\n$$"] [17592186128932 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to -3} \\sqrt[3]{3x-2x^2}.\n$$"] [17592186128932 :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=-3$ into the expression, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  - 3}\\sqrt[3]{3x - 2x^{2}} &= \\sqrt[3]{3(-3) - 2(-3)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt[3]{-9 - 18} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt[3]{-27} \\\\\n&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128967 :question/worked-solution "Here, we have a rational inequality that is non-zero on one side. So, we rewrite the inequality to give zero on one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{x-4} & <x \\\\ \\frac{2x}{x-4}-x & <0 \\\\ \\frac{2x-x(x-4)}{x-4} & <0 \\\\ \\frac{-{x}^{2}+6x}{x-4} & <0 \\\\ \\frac{x(6-x)}{x-4} & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed using the sign table method. First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x(6-x)}{x-4}\n$$\n\nSo, we're solving the inequality $f(x) < 0$.\n\nThe solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nare $x=0$ and $x=6$. Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=4$. The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,0), \\quad (0,4), \\quad (4,6), \\quad (6, \\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(-\\infty,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(0,4)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(4,6)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(6, \\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6-x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo solve the inequality $f(x) < 0$, we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(0, 4)$ and $(6,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the solution to $f(x) < 0$ is\n$x \\in (0,4) \\cup (6, \\infty)$."] [17592186128969 :question/worked-solution "Here, we have a rational inequality that is non-zero on one side. So, we rewrite the inequality to give zero on one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{x+2} & >\\frac{x}{x-2} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{x+2}-\\frac{x}{x-2} & >0 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}-x(x+2)}{(x+2)(x-2)} & >0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-4x+4-{x}^{2}-2x}{(x+2)(x-2)} & >0 \\\\ \\frac{4-6x}{(x+2)(x-2)} & >0 \\\\ \\frac{2(2-3x)}{(x+2)(x-2)} & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed using the sign table method. First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{2(2-3x)}{(x+2)(x-2)}\n$$\n\nSo, we're now solving the inequality $f(x) > 0$.\n\nThe solution to the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nAlso, $f(x)$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=-2$ and $x=2$. The root and asymptotes divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,-2), \\quad \\left(-2,\\frac{2}{3}\\right), \\quad \\left(\\frac{2}{3},2\\right), \\quad (2,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(-\\infty,-2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(-2,\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3},2\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(2,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2-3x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x+2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo solve the inequality $f(x) > 0$, we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals\n\n$$\n\\left(-2,\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3},2\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the solution to $f(x) > 0$ is\n\n$$\nx \\in (-\\infty,-2)\\cup\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3},2\\right)\n$$"] [17592186129023 :question/problem "Compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\sqrt[3]{x-1}.\n$$"] [17592186129023 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\sqrt[3]{x-1}\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n![](images/04/0481bb8a4e6058d3cc41069a210f7fc964d861c7071541432aa7422349410431.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} f(x)= \\infty\n$$"] [17592186129455 :question/problem "Consider the following derivation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & (x+3)^{2}-6x & = \\\\ {\\color{gray}{\\text{Step 1:}}} &  & ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot x\\cdot 3+9)-6x & = \\\\ {\\color{gray}{\\text{Step 2:}}} &  & ({x}^{2}+6x+9)-6x & = \\\\ {\\color{gray}{\\text{Step 3:}}} &  & {x}^{2}+(6x-6x)+9 & = \\\\ {\\color{gray}{\\text{Step 4:}}} &  & {x}^{2}+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements is true?"] [17592186129456 :question/problem "Consider the following derivation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & 5x-(x-5)^{2} & = \\\\ {\\color{gray}{\\text{Step 1:}}} &  & 5x-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot x\\cdot 5+25) & = \\\\ {\\color{gray}{\\text{Step 2:}}} &  & 5x-({x}^{2}+5x+25) & = \\\\ {\\color{gray}{\\text{Step 3:}}} &  & -{x}^{2}+(5x-5x)-25 & = \\\\ {\\color{gray}{\\text{Step 4:}}} &  & -{x}^{2}-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements is true?"] [17592186129553 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x \\to  8^{+}}(\\sqrt[3]{x} + 1)$."] [17592186129595 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}\\sqrt{5 + 4x}$."] [17592186129723 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  4^{-}}\\sqrt{x - 4}$."] [17592186129788 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}\\sqrt{x}$?"] [17592186129789 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}\\sqrt[3]{x}$?"] [17592186129790 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  3}\\sqrt[3]{2x - 6}$."] [17592186129791 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}\\sqrt[3]{5x + 3}$?"] [17592186129794 :question/problem "Given the functions $f(x)$, $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x =-3$?\n\n$\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}8+x, & x<-3 \\\\ 2-x, & x\\ge -3,\\end{cases} \\\\ g(x) & =\\begin{cases}7+x, & x<-3 \\\\ 3-x, & x\\ge -3,\\end{cases} \\\\ h(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}(x+3).\\end{aligned}$."] [17592186129795 :question/problem "Given the functions $f(x), g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a point discontinuity at $x = 0$?\n$\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}{e}^{x}, & x\\le 0 \\\\ 1, & x>0\\end{cases} \\\\  \\\\ g(x) & =\\begin{cases}2x+3, & x<0 \\\\ 1, & x=0 \\\\ -3x+3, & x>0\\end{cases} \\\\  \\\\ h(x) & =\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}+4 & x<0 \\\\ 4, & x=0 \\\\ 4(x+1), & x>0\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}$"] [17592186129797 :question/problem "Given the functions $f(x), g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x = 2$?\n\n$\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}\\frac{2}{x-2}, & x<2 \\\\ x+1, & x\\ge 2,\\end{cases} \\\\ g(x) & =\\begin{cases}2-{x}^{2}, & x\\le 2 \\\\ x+1, & x>2,\\end{cases} \\\\ h(x) & =\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}-3x, & x<2 \\\\ -3, & x\\ge 2,\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}$,"] [17592186129818 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  4}\\sqrt{6x + 1}$."] [17592186129836 :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =\\begin{cases}5x-5, & x<1 \\\\ 0, & x=1 \\\\ {x}^{3}-1, & x>1\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186129836 :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(1)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}(5x-5)=5(1)-5=0, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}({x}^{3}-1)={1}^{3}-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x)$ is true and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}g(x)=0.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is $\\lim_{x\\to 1}g(x)=0,$ and the value of the function is $g(1)=0.$ So, statement $\\lim_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)$ is true and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, the function is continuous at $x=0,$ and statement “$g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$” is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)$ are true."] [17592186129849 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}6x,\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,-1<y<0, \\\\ {e}^{x+y},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,0<y<1,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$"] [17592186129873 :question/problem "![](images/ed/ed0be2aca8d43d733e91f3a8feaaca0649957dcbd516a8218b1e7dea136170d8.png)\n\nConsider the region $R$ shown above. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}x, & (x,y)\\in S, \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in T, \\\\ {x}^{2}y, & (x,y)\\in U.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$"] [17592186130368 :tutorial/content "We can use the sign table method to solve rational inequalities with zero on one side. For example, the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{x+2} > 0\n$$\n\ncan be solved using the sign table method.\n\nSometimes, rational inequalities require some rearrangement before we can apply the sign table method.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{x+2} > x\n$$\n\nTo solve this inequality, we first subtract $x$ from both sides of the inequality to get zero on one side:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{x+2} - x > 0\n$$\n\nThen, we put all of the terms on the left-hand side over a common denominator and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{x+2}-x & >0 \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{x+2}-\\frac{x(x+2)}{x+2} & >0 \\\\ \\frac{x+1-x(x+2)}{x+2} & >0 \\\\ \\frac{x+1-{x}^{2}-2x}{x+2} & >0 \\\\ \\frac{1-x-{x}^{2}}{x+2} & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality using the sign table method."] [17592186130382 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we claimed that function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$ Let's now justify this claim.\n\nRecall that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=x_0$ with derivative $f'(x_0)$ if and only if the following limit exists:\n\n$$\nf'(x_0) = \\lim_{x\\to x_0}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(x_0)} {x-x_0}\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this limit as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {x}_{0}}\\left(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}}\\right)-{f}^{′}({x}_{0}) & =0 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {x}_{0}}(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}}-{f}^{′}({x}_{0})) & =0 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {x}_{0}}\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})-(x-{x}_{0}){f}^{′}({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the above limit to hold true, the numerator must approach zero. So, near $x_0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) - (x-x_0)f'(x_0) \\approx 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) \\approx (x-x_0)f'(x_0).\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following equation of the tangent line at $x_0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny -y_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x}(x_0)\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can say that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$\n\nWe'll now use a similar idea to define the differentiability of a multivariable function at a point $P$ in terms of tangent planes."] [17592186130448 :tutorial/content "Let's compute the distance between the following complex numbers:\n\n$$\nz_{1} = 1 + \\textrm{i}, z_{2} = 2 + 3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nComputing the difference, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz_{1} - z_{2} &= (1 + \\textrm{i}) - (2 + 3\\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&=-1 - 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the numbers is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{z}_{1}-{z}_{2}| & =|-1-2\\text{i}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133543 :question/worked-solution "Applying a stretch of factor $k$ and invariant $x$-axis to a point means that the $y$-coordinate of the point should be multiplied by $k$.\n\nThe following function represents this transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(x,ky\\right)\n$$\n\nSince the points $P(1,6)$ and $Q(9,8)$ are mapped to the points $P'(1,3)$ and $Q'(9,4)$, respectively, by the stretch, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1,6) & ↦(1,k\\cdot 6)=(1,3), \\\\ (9,8) & ↦(9,k\\cdot 8)=(9,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1, 6k) &= (1, 3), \\\\\n(9, 8k) &= (9, 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $y$-coordinates, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6k=3 & \\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}k=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ 8k=4 & \\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}k=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134150 :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{2}{21}x,\\, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0,\\, & x<2 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>5\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186134150 :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $2\\le x\\le 5,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<2 \\\\ {\\int }_{2}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1, & x>5\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{x}\\frac{2}{21}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={\\frac{1}{21}{t}^{2}|}_{2}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}{x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0,\\, & x<2, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{21},\\, & 2\\le x\\le 5, \\\\ 1,\\, & x>5.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186134158 :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}6{e}^{-6x},\\, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186134158 :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge 0,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}6{e}^{-6t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & =-{e}^{-6t}{|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-6x}+1 \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<0, \\\\ 1-{e}^{-6x}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186134221 :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x = [x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}]^{T}$, extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of $R^{3}$. What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|?$?\n$$\n\\displaystyle U = {\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$"] [17592186134538 :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<1\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$"] [17592186134538 :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge 1,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 1\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={-\\frac{1}{t}|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<1, \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{x}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186135094 :tutorial/content "A fair coin is tossed, then a fair die is rolled. What is the probability of the coin landing on heads and the die landing on $5$?\n\nLet's call $H$ the event \"the coin lands on heads,\" and $F$ is the event \"the die lands on $5$\". Notice that $H$ and $F$ are independent events since the outcome of one event doesn't influence the other. Let's use a tree diagram to compute the required probability.\n\nThe probabilities on the first level of the tree correspond to the event $H$:\n\n$$\nP(H) = \\dfrac12, \\qquad P(H') = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nLet's add these values to our diagram.\n\n![](images/43/431ab148431d5a89d1c60bccebf71310e086f96009c7d39c438445e374f32ef4.png)\n\nNext, we find the probabilities on the second level. Since $H$ and $F$ are independent, these correspond to the probability of $F$ occurring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|H) & =P(F|{H}^{'})=P(F)={\\color{blue}{\\frac{1}{6}}} \\\\ P({F}^{'}|H) & =P({F}^{'}|{H}^{'})=P({F}^{'})={\\color{red}{\\frac{5}{6}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram:\n\n![](images/10/10af4ec660a95ddedbaa706fe2e2b35a37735b5708926d3b4042ef319542e32c.png)\n\nFinally, to compute the event that the coin lands on heads and the die lands on five, we multiply across the branches:\n\n![](images/07/07bb6d137efaa12079c2a68566fcce0876d586d0eaae9da911c68fc1422df172.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(H\\cap F) = \\dfrac12\\cdot\\dfrac16 = \\dfrac{1}{12}\n$$"] [17592186135189 :tutorial/content "A **multiple** of $\\color{blue}2$ is any number that can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} \\times \\boxed{ {\\phantom{A}}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{A}}}$ is a whole number.\n\nFor example, let's take a look at the first few entries of the ${\\color{blue}{2}}\\times$ multiplication table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rcl}{\\color{blue}{2}}\\times 1 & = & {\\color{red}{2}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}}\\times 2 & = & {\\color{red}{4}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}}\\times 3 & = & {\\color{red}{6}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}}\\times 4 & = & {\\color{red}{8}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}}\\times 5 & = & {\\color{red}{10}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe multiples of $\\color{blue}2$ are given by the following products:\n${\\color{red}2}, \\quad {\\color{red}4}, \\quad {\\color{red}6}, \\quad {\\color{red}8}, \\quad {\\color{red}10}, \\quad \\ldots$\n\nWe can picture all of the multiples of $\\color{blue}2$ using a number line:\n\n![](images/42/4211337d656766e5964015789e7508386aa59e81284268b3a8722e7442bed400.png)"] [17592186138092 :question/worked-solution "To estimate the total amount that Michael paid, we need to estimate the product $13 \\times 17$.\n\nWe round both factors to the nearest ten:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}13 & \\times  & 17 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{10}} & \\times  & {\\color{blue}{20}}\\end{array}\n$$\nSo, we now need to find the value of $10 \\times 20$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}10 & ⟶10 \\\\ 20 & ⟶20\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{1} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2} = \\color{red}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n${\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}00}$\n\nTherefore, Michael paid approximately $\\$200$ for the T-shirts."] [17592186138093 :question/worked-solution "To estimate the total number of tools produced, we need to estimate the product $694 \\times 38$.\n\nWe round both factors to the nearest ten, as required:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}694 & \\times  & 38 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{690}} & \\times  & {\\color{blue}{40}}\\end{array}\n$$\nHence, we need to find the value of $690 \\times 40$.\n\nFirst, we figure out non-zero parts and blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}690 & ⟶690 \\\\ 40 & ⟶40\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts. We can do this using an area model:\n\n![](images/fa/faa4a2b25fbbee9445b34bbbc9054e140bd48a6ffc296f71d37afdbaf4a35837.png)\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n69\\times 4 = 240+36 = {\\color{red}{276}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}276}{\\color{blue}00} = 27,600\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tools factory produced approximately $27,600$ tools."] [17592186138094 :question/worked-solution "We round the first factor to the nearest ten and the second factor to the nearest hundred:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}23 & \\times  & 785 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{20}} & \\times  & {\\color{blue}{800}}\\end{array}\n$$\nHence, we need to find the value of $20 \\times 800$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}20 & ⟶20 \\\\ 800 & ⟶800\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $3$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{8} = \\color{red}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}16}{\\color{blue}000} = 16,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, $23 \\times 785$ is approximately $16,000$."] [17592186138095 :question/worked-solution "We round the first factor to the nearest thousand and the second factor to the nearest ten:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}3,586 & \\times  & 43 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{4,000}} & \\times  & {\\color{blue}{40}}\\end{array}\n$$\nHence, we need to find the value of $4,000 \\times 40$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}4, 000 & ⟶4000 \\\\ 40 & ⟶40\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $4$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{4} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{4} = \\color{red}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}16}{\\color{blue}0000} = 160,000\n$$\n\nSo, the estimate is $160,000$."] [17592186138142 :question/worked-solution "We round both factors to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}7.7 & \\times  & 5.2 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{8}} & \\times  & {\\color{blue}{5}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of $8 \\times 5$. Computing the product, we get:\n\n$$\n8 \\times 5 = 40\n$$\n\nTherefore, $7.7 \\times 5.2$ is approximately $40$."] [17592186138143 :question/worked-solution "We round both factors to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}6.15 & \\times  & 8.64 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{6}} & \\times  & {\\color{blue}{9}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of $6 \\times 9$. Computing the product, we get:\n\n$$\n6 \\times 9 = 54\n$$\n\nTherefore, $6.15 \\times 8.64$ is approximately $54$."] [17592186138154 :question/worked-solution "There is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $15.4$ and there are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $20.79$.\n\nIn order to add the two numbers, we need them to have the same number of decimal places. So, we rewrite the first number so that it has $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n15.4=15.4{\\color{red}0}\n$$\n\nNext, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}1\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0